<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maha Yazawin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1724</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Maha Yazawin-gyi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maha Yazawin (Great Chronicle) is the first national chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, compiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-kala">U Kala</a> and completed around 1724 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo dynasty</a>. It traces Burmese kingship from legendary Buddhist origins to the early 18th century and became the basis for the later Konbaung chronicles, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin">Hmannan Yazawin</a> of 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin.jpg" alt="Maha Yazawin" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maha_Yazawin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
